From 0d5809e7cd08e2a0bd6c2b0a90812bb156e0fd92 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 18:36:58 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10811/11171] The deferred restart was unnecessary: the
 conductor re-execs on fresh source

The conductor re-execs itself when committed source changes
(REQ-CONDUCTOR-FRESHEXEC-1, research_conductor.py:2402) and did so at 21:50Z,
logged as e372551c2942 -> d615af6127c7. My both-files-required fix was committed at
21:13Z, so it was in that source. Verified rather than assumed: the current file's
sha256 prefix is d615af6127c7, matching the re-exec target exactly.

So the .605 falsification test is valid as written and the deferred restart is moot.
The re-exec is guarded too, skipping when HEAD does not compile or import, so a
half-written edit cannot re-exec the conductor into broken code.

The entry also records the trap this exposed. A re-exec replaces the process image
while keeping the PID, so ps -o lstart still reports the original start time --
15:06Z here, hours before the source actually running. I reasoned from that start
time across three hourly checks and concluded "the fix is still unloaded" every
time. Process start time cannot answer which source a long-lived process is running.
Compare the file hash against the last re-exec line in ops/conductor-log.md, or grep
the running file for the change.

